#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106910} :activity/id #uuid "df06df55-18c1-571d-8ff5-d8bef7d927dd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049261 :topic/id #uuid "e7607418-447d-5293-a591-393372350d62" :topic/math-academy-id 1335} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106910 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107123 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f_X$ and support\n\n$$\nS_X = \\{-1,0,1\\}\n$$\n\nshown below.\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ |\n\nNow suppose that we define a new random variable $Y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nY = (X+1)^3\n$$\n\nComputing $\\textrm{E}[Y]$ is quite a lot of work if we use only the methods we've seen up to now. Typically, we would need to find the probability mass function $f_Y$ of $Y$, and then compute $\\textrm{E}[Y]$.\n\nThankfully, there is a handy rule, called **the rule of the lazy statistician,** that allows us to compute $\\textrm{E}[Y]$ *without* having to find $f_Y$ first! This is useful because $\\textrm{E}[Y]$ might be the only quantity we're interested in.\n\nThe rule of the lazy statistician states that if $Y = r(X)$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[Y] = \\sum_{x \\in S_X}r(x) \\, f_X(x)\n$$\n\nApplying the rule of the lazy statistician with the function\n\n$$\nr(x) = (x+1)^3\n$$\n\nfor our PMF $f_X$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= ∑_(x ∈ S_{X})r(x)f_{X}(x) \\\\\n&= ∑_(x ∈ S_{X})(x + 1)^{3}f_{X}(x) \\\\\n&= (-1 + 1)^{3} \\cdot 0.25 + (0 + 1)^{3} \\cdot 0.5 + (1 + 1)^{3} \\cdot 0.25 \\\\\n&= 0^{3} \\cdot 0.25 + 1^{3} \\cdot 0.5 + 2^{3} \\cdot 0.25 \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 0.25 + 1 \\cdot 0.5 + 8 \\cdot 0.25 \\\\\n&= 0 + 0.5 + 2 \\\\\n&= 2.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[Y] = 2.5\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dba36f3b-5f7a-5e24-861a-b5635908f917" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5535 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "49816a73-0431-5661-9a70-b0f19f749956" :step/math-academy-id 453122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106911}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106911 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107134 :tutorial/content "The rule of the lazy statistician states that if $Y = r(X)$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[Y] = \\sum_{x \\in S_X}r(x) \\, f_X(x)\n$$\n\nNote the following points:\n\n- The reason for the name \"the rule of the lazy statistician\" is that the rule is sometimes mistaken as a definition when, in fact, it is a statement that requires rigorous proof (we won't do that here).\n- The rule is sometimes called the **law of the unconscious statistician** (or **LOTUS**).\n- In general $\\textrm E[r(X)] \\ne r(\\textrm E[X])$.\n\nThe rule has some nice intuition behind it. We demonstrate with a simple example.\n\nSuppose a spinner has four sides, labeled $1$ through $4$. Let the random variable $X$ equal the result when the spinner is spun once, and let the function $Y = r(X)$ denote the profit made when the spinner lands on $X = x$. To calculate the *expected* profit on each spin, we multiply each possible payout by the probability of that payout occurring and sum over all possible values of $X$.\n\nLet's take a look at another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "cfe6f3bd-33c7-5c0b-989d-7e0e0716bb9e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6323 :tutorial/title "Some Comments"} :step/id #uuid "4a3e29fd-cad9-5476-bac7-05f605eb832a" :step/math-academy-id 453123 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106912}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106912 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105900 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105688 :question/id #uuid "8ffc2485-0297-59f4-95cc-365c20c72f65" :question/math-academy-id "e-9216"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8a1ff93-7caf-5f2a-98fa-8f8ab77f9ec8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048317}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106030}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Rule of the Lazy Statistician for Discrete Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "2d6ff61a-b40a-5779-8e0d-7e8ab94360bf" :step/math-academy-id 453124 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106913}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106913 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107125 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the probability density function $f(x)$ and let $Y=r(X)$. Then, the rule of the lazy statistician for continuous random variables states that\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[Y]=\\textrm E[r(X)]=\\int^{\\infty}_{-\\infty} r(x)f(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nNotice the analogy with the discrete case." :tutorial/id #uuid "f9892132-3602-5dbf-a92b-6cfbab1207f8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5563 :tutorial/title "The Rule of the Lazy Statistician for Continuous Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "cd61e0e8-6780-536a-a264-b2f23461b6a3" :step/math-academy-id 453127 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106914}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106914 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105901 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105689 :question/id #uuid "da07f9ee-69bf-5c73-8152-e99023fff8c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9217"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa712994-dace-59ea-aaec-cc83ad4fb4a6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049801}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106032}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Rule of the Lazy Statistician for Continuous Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "f7818225-2845-544f-834c-59feb1e8d177" :step/math-academy-id 453128 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106915}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106915 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105902 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105516 :question/id #uuid "e0bc63c6-b39e-56b2-ada5-1b30bd659b84" :question/math-academy-id "e-18765"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b181e0ec-2117-5c97-8b6a-dda79e5e1168" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106272}] :knowledge-point/title "Applications of LOTUS"} :step/id #uuid "0e944ae9-96c9-5107-81b1-4d0a094457be" :step/math-academy-id 453131 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106916}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106916 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107124 :tutorial/content "Although\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[r(X)] \\ne r(\\textrm{E}[X])\n$$\n\nin general, equality is possible for some functions.\n\nFor example, we've already seen that if $a$ and $b$ are real constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[aX+b]=a \\, \\textrm{E}[X]+b\n$$\n\nLet's prove this using the rule of the lazy statistician for a discrete random variable $X$. In this case,\n\n$$\nr(X)=aX+b\n$$\n\n, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[aX + b] &= ∑_(x ∈ S)(ax + b)f(x) \\\\\n&= ∑_(x ∈ S)axf(x) + ∑_(x ∈ S)bf(x) \\\\\n&= a∑_(x ∈ S)xf(x) + b∑_(x ∈ S)f(x) \\\\\n&= aE[X] + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA similar proof can be conducted for continuous random variables.\n\nSimilarly, for functions $r(x)$ and $s(x)$ and real coefficients $a$ and $b$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[a \\cdot r(X) + b \\cdot s(X)] = a \\, \\textrm{E}[r(X)] + b \\, \\textrm{E}[s(X)]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "41c8d9b6-bbfa-525a-bafd-ce7949043e4a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5560 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Linearity of Expectation"} :step/id #uuid "3f442870-7bcf-5929-9ad4-d5ca7c6e4de1" :step/math-academy-id 453134}] :activity/title "The Rule of the Lazy Statistician" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105978}]}
